      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im having a really hard time with my signals class. i get the formulas for the fourier transform but i dont really get the actual intuition. like how can one single wave just be made up of a bunch of other waves? can u explain it in a way that actually makes sense?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Imagine a complex sound, like a full orchestra playing a piece. That single, rich sound wave is like one of those tricky, complicated signals you're studying. The Fourier Transform, in essence, is like a highly skilled band of specialized sound engineers who can take that one big, messy sound and break it down into all the individual pure tones—the fundamental frequencies and their overtones—that make it up. It's not that the original wave *is* those smaller waves, but rather that it's a precise, weighted sum of them. Think of it like a recipe: the final cake (the complex wave) is made up of flour, sugar, eggs, and butter (the constituent sine and cosine waves).",
